# The Survey - the other side of The Canteen: the lad who ate the pie, and the summer he stopped.
# Written for the library. The first story here that pairs with another, and the first whose subject is a kindness
# he did not acknowledge at the time.

title: The Survey
start: start

== start ==
bg east_corner
music room_tone
You are eighteen and you have a clipboard and a hi-vis that is too big, and the
job is a survey: levels, levels, levels, all summer, four sites.
Your father got you the work. He is the inspector, which means he walks through
buildings with a different clipboard and says no to things, and on site nobody
talks to you unless the talking is about the levels.
-> the_site

== the_site ==
bg dining
The canteen is the only place on the site where you are not a clipboard. It
serves eleven to two, it has a woman on the till who never asks your name, and on
Wednesdays there is pie.
You go in at ten past two because that is when the queue has gone and the food
is still out and you can sit at the end table under the clock with your back to
the room.
The first Wednesday you ask for the pie and the woman says, before you have
finished the sentence: no onion. You do not tell her that you hate onion. You
just eat it.
-> the_wednesdays

== the_wednesdays ==
It becomes a thing that happens. Wednesday, ten past two, the end table, the
small pie with no onion in it, and eleven minutes, which is how long you take.
You do not know her name and she does not know yours. You never ask for the no
onion again and she never asks you about it. It is the only hour of the week you
are not being looked at.
-> the_end_of_the_summer

== the_end_of_the_summer ==
bg east_corner
The survey finishes in August. You hand in the clipboard, you are paid for
eleven weeks, and the Tuesday after that you go back to college and stop going
past the site, because there is no reason to and it is four miles out of the way.
In October your father says the site is moving in the spring, in the way he says
things about buildings: as a fact about the building and not about anybody.
* Say something about the canteen. -> say_something
* Say nothing, and put the site out of your head. -> say_nothing
* Go back on a Wednesday, on the bus, without telling anyone. -> go_back

== say_something ==
you: There's a woman in the canteen. She used to do me a pie with no onion in it.
father: What?
you: Nothing. She did me a pie.
He looks at you the way he looks at a level that does not match the drawing, and
then he says the fact about the moving again, in almost the same words, because
that is how he deals with things that are not about buildings.
-> the_bus

== say_nothing ==
You say nothing. There is nothing to say: a woman made you a pie for eleven weeks
because you asked for one the first time and she remembered, and that is not a
story, that is a canteen.
-> the_bus

== go_back ==
bg dining
You go back on a Wednesday in November and it takes an hour and a bus change and
you get there at ten past two, which is the exact minute, because of course it
is.
The canteen looks the same. The end table is there. Under the clock. You stand in
the doorway with your hands out of your pockets and the woman on the till looks
up and says:
woman: No onion?
And you have not been in this building for three months and she has a queue of
two behind you, and she remembers, and you realise you have been carrying
something for eleven weeks that you did not know you were carrying.
* Say yes and sit down. -> sit_down
* Say that you are not staying, and thank her properly anyway. -> thank_her
* Say nothing, and go. -> leave_quiet

== the_bus ==
The bus is forty minutes and you sit at the top of it with your coat on your
knees, and somewhere over the ring road you think about the eleven weeks and
about how many Wednesdays that actually is, which is eleven, and about the fact
that you never once said thank you in a way that was about anything.
You are eighteen. You have your whole life, and one pie, and no idea yet that
the second one is the one you will keep.
-> ending_quiet

== sit_down ==
You sit at the end table under the clock. She brings the small pie over herself,
which she does not have to do, and puts it down and does not stay, and you eat it
in eleven minutes because that is how long you take.
When you go up to pay she has already put it through and she will not take the
money, and you say thank you in the doorway like a person and not like a
clipboard, and the bell does what bells do.
-> END

== thank_her ==
You say you are not staying and that you came out on the bus to say thank you,
which you have owed her for eleven weeks, and that you did not know it was owed
until you were standing in the doorway.
She looks at you for a moment. Then she says the thing that ends it:
woman: It's what the card says. It was up before you came in.
-> ending_on_the_card

== leave_quiet ==
You say nothing. You let the door shut behind you and you walk to the stop and
you are on the bus home by four, and that is the last you see of her, and there
is a version of this where that is a shame and there is the version where it is
simply what eighteen is.
-> ending_quiet

== ending_on_the_card ==
There was a strip of card on the board, in the same marker as the day's menu, and
it said *for R. no onion* in somebody's writing, and it had been up since the
summer.
You do not find out that she kept making it every Wednesday after you stopped
coming. You find that out a long time later, from nobody in particular, the way
you find out about things that happened in a room you were in.
-> END

== ending_quiet ==
You are twenty-six before you tell anybody about the eleven weeks, and when you
tell it the person says it is a nice story about a canteen, which is what it is.
You have a job now. You still do not like onion. You have never once had a pie
with onion in it and you have never asked anybody why that is.
-> END
